    "content": "Former Somali Custodial Corps commander General Mahad Abdirahman, also known as Shub, has stated that he survived an airstrike on his residence in the border town of Dolow. In an interview with the Geed Fadhi podcast, Abdirahman said the attack occurred at midnight on September 3. He recounted hearing an F-16 aircraft flying over his home before two missiles struck, one landing near his head and another in his bedroom. He did not identify the country operating the jet.\n\nThe general's claim comes amid a complex security landscape in Somalia, where airstrikes have been conducted by various international forces, including the United States and Kenya, targeting militant groups like al-Shabaab. Dolow, located near the Ethiopian and Kenyan borders, has seen its share of instability, though direct attacks on high-profile figures are rare. Abdirahman served as the head of the Custodial Corps, a branch of the Somali military responsible for prisons, until his dismissal in 2017. His tenure was marked by efforts to reform the corps amid ongoing conflict.\n\nThe Somali Guardian could not independently verify the general's account, and no group or government has claimed responsibility for the strike. The incident raises questions about the presence and operations of foreign military aircraft in Somali airspace, particularly in border regions where multiple actors are active. The lack of official comment from Somali authorities or international partners leaves the circumstances unclear.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this event underscores the persistent volatility in parts of Somalia, even as the federal government attempts to stabilize the country. Many in the diaspora have family members in border towns like Dolow and closely follow security developments. The possibility of a foreign airstrike on a former senior official could heighten concerns about accountability and the risks faced by those who return to serve in Somalia. It also highlights the challenges of verifying events on the ground, a common issue for diaspora communities relying on fragmented news sources.\n\nThe general's survival, if confirmed, would be a notable episode in a region where airstrikes have often resulted in civilian casualties. It may prompt calls for greater transparency from both Somali and international military forces operating in the country. As of now, no further details have emerged about the motive or the parties involved.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somali-general-says-he-survived-f16-strike/",
